    "content": {
      "component": "blog_article",
      "sharepoint_id": "87",
      "title": "What does it mean to be a Climate Advocate?",
      "description": "",
      "date": "Jul 31, 2025",
      "read_time": "5 min read",
      "author": "Didar Ali",
      "isGlobal": true,
      "banner_image": {
        "fieldtype": "asset",
        "_local": "87-Banner.png"
      },
      "thumbnail": {
        "fieldtype": "asset"
      },
      "languages": [],
      "show_share": true,
      "show_similar": true,
      "similar_show_header": true,
      "category": "",
      "isFeatured": null,
      "body": [
        {
          "component": "rich_text",
          "content": {
            "type": "doc",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "I wasn't holding a placard the first time I realized I had to do something about the climate crisis. I wasn't on a panel or in a protest. I was standing near a stream in my village that had fed our valley for years. In the last decade, the stream had been causing devastation with increased flooding year after year."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In my hometown in the high mountains of Gilgit-Baltistan - a region home to over 7,000 glaciers, the largest number outside the Arctic and Antarctic and often called the \"Third Pole\" - spoke about how the seasons no longer made sense. How water arrived too late or disappeared too early. At first, I didn't know what to do with all that worry. I just knew it wasn't enough to stay quiet."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Most people think a climate advocate or activist is someone always speaking at global summits, leading movements, or being featured in the media. But the truth is, most climate activists are doing work that goes unseen. They're organizing school workshops, clean-up drives, debate national policies, protesting, translating climate knowledge into local languages, working with communities, cleaning rivers, or just starting conversations that matter."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The climate crisis is enormous. But not every response has to be. What matters is that we show up with care, honesty, and consistency. That's what I've learned over the past few years, not from a textbook, but through lived experience. I've worked with youth-led climate organizations, joined climate camps in Chitral and Balochistan, interned at research centers in Central Asia and now in East Africa, and worked closely with young people back home in Gilgit-Baltistan."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "My first step wasn't fancy. I joined a local clean-up campaign. Picking up trash might seem minor when glaciers are melting and floods are destroying homes, but it was my way in. From there, I started reading more, writing about what I learned, joining student-led climate groups, and slowly finding people who cared about the same things."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "That's the thing - climate activism doesn't begin with a viral campaign. It begins with small actions, taken seriously. It starts in classrooms, WhatsApp groups, local parks, and yes, in quiet worry by a stream. If you want to begin, begin where you are. Pick up a book. Join a club. Volunteer for a small event. You don't need to have all the answers. You just need to care enough to ask the right questions."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "For a year, I worked in Chitral teaching youth about environmental conservation. Later, I joined one of Pakistan's leading climate organizations, the Climate Forward Pakistan (CFP), where I had the chance to grow further and learn from people across the country."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "One of the most powerful moments in my journey was at a youth-led climate camp in coastal Balochistan. I met young people from different regions and backgrounds. Some were facing drought, others rising seas. Some had never been asked what they thought about climate change but all of them had stories."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "That's when it clicked for me. Activism isn't just about fighting for the planet. It's about people. It's about justice. Climate change is not just an environmental issue, it's about inequality, displacement, food, water, health, and dignity. So, if you want to become a climate activist, start by finding people. Sit with elders. Listen to farmers. Work with students. Talk to girls who want to make their voices heard. Movements grow through connection, not competition."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "During my internships, I worked on climate research projects, tracking fossil fuel developments, reviewing policy documents, and mapping climate trends. But I quickly realized something. Research on its own isn't enough. If people can't access or understand the knowledge, it doesn't help them."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "That's why I believe our job as activists isn't to act like we know everything. It's to listen. The people we often consider \"illiterate\" are sometimes the ones who carry the deepest Indigenous Knowledge. They've been protecting the Earth long before we had terms like climate resilience. One thing we can do is translate key climate ideas into local languages, turn reports into simple visuals, and create spaces for open discussion. We don't need more complicated data. We need clearer ways to share what we already know, and learn more."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Let's be real. Climate activists often get criticised. \"You're just doing this for recognition,\" people say. \"You want to go to conferences and win awards.\" I've heard it. Maybe you have too."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "But for many of us, the work is deeply personal. It's unpaid, often unrecognized, and done on the side of everything else in life. We do it because we care. Because we're scared. Because we see what's happening to our people and our lands and we refuse to stay silent."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "There's also a deeper reason why this work matters. British Philosopher & Ethicist Elizabeth Cripps writes about the no-harm principle which states that each of us has a moral duty not to cause serious harm to others, including future generations. When it comes to climate change, personal lifestyle changes aren't enough. The problem is too big, too systemic. The only way to prevent harm is through collective action. That means organizing, resisting, building, and yes, activism."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "You don't have to lead a movement to be part of one. You just have to join in."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "So, what does it mean to be a climate activist?"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "It means picking up the plastic bottle and the microphone. It means reading quietly and speaking loudly. It means showing up, again and again, whether or not anyone is watching."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "You don't need to be perfect. You just need to be present. Climate activism isn't a title you earn it's a way of showing up in the world. "
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "I never imagined that a stream would be my turning point. But it was. And now, I carry that moment with me everywhere I go."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Maybe your turning point hasn't come yet. Or maybe it already has, and you just haven't named it."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Either way, if you care enough to do something, you're already part of this."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "And the Earth needs every single one of us."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
